# JSON text read into a `Value` and a `Value` written as JSON text (RFC 8259),
# where a text that is not JSON is a failure the caller answers and never an
# abort. Go's `json.Marshal` and `json.Unmarshal` fill and read a program's own
# types through reflection, which Heroes has no route for, so the contract is
# another and so are the names: `parse` and `render` over one `Value`, a
# variant a program matches.
#
# `parse` and `render` walk a value with loops, and no function calling
# itself: a function called for each level would stand on this face, which
# declares only what a program calls (`examples/packages/README.md` § 1.2),
# and could not stand under `internal/`, whose modules cannot name `Value`
# without using this one, a cycle the language refuses. The arrays and objects
# still open are kept in arrays beside each loop instead. Both stop at a
# nesting of 512, so a program's own walk of a value, a call per level, stays
# inside its stack. The tokens, strings and numbers are read by the modules
# under `encoding/json/internal/`.

use encoding/json/internal/numeral
use encoding/json/internal/quoted
use encoding/json/internal/tokens

# What `parse` fails with where a text is not JSON, and `render` where a
# number is not finite, which no JSON text writes.
constant NOT_JSON: str
    "not_json"

# What `parse` fails with where arrays and objects nest deeper than 512, and
# `render` where a value does.
constant TOO_DEEP: str
    "too_deep"

# What `field` fails with where the value it is asked of is no object.
constant NOT_AN_OBJECT: str
    "not_an_object"

# A JSON value, one of JSON's six kinds: `nothing` is JSON's `null`, a word
# Heroes refuses. An object's `fields` keep its names in the order the text
# wrote them, which `keys` gives, and two objects holding the same fields are
# equal in any order (spec § 7).
variant Value
    nothing
    boolean
        value: bool
    number
        value: f64
    string
        value: str
    array
        items: [Value]
    object
        fields: {str: Value}

# The value `s` holds as JSON text, white space allowed around it and nothing
# else after it. Each number is the `f64` nearest the number written, and a
# name written twice in one object keeps the value written last. It fails
# `not_json` where `s` is not JSON, `too_deep` where arrays and objects nest
# deeper than 512, `does_not_fit` where a number lies past the largest `f64`,
# and `not_text` where a `\u` escape names half of a surrogate pair alone.
function parse(s: str) -> Value?
    r: tokens.Reader @= tokens.start(s)

    # The arrays and objects open around the reader, outermost first, each with
    # what it holds so far: its elements, or its fields and the name its next
    # value takes. The innermost stands apart in `items`, `fields` and `name`,
    # so a value joins it in place: `push` on an array reached through an index
    # copies the whole array (spec § 10).
    outer_items: [[Value]] @= []
    outer_fields: [{str: Value}] @= []
    outer_names: [str] @= []
    outer_objects: [bool] @= []
    items: [Value] @= []
    fields: {str: Value} @= {}
    name: str @= ""
    in_object: bool @= false
    whole: Value @= .nothing

    while true
        token = tokens.next(@r)?
        made: Value @= .nothing
        closing: bool @= false

        match token
            .open_array | .open_object =>
                outer_items @ outer_items.push(items)
                outer_fields @ outer_fields.push(fields)
                outer_names @ outer_names.push(name)
                outer_objects @ outer_objects.push(in_object)
                items @ []
                fields @ {}
                in_object @ token == .open_object
                continue
            .name n =>
                name @ n.text
                continue
            .end =>
                return ok(whole)
            .close_array =>
                made @ .array(items: items)
                closing @ true
            .close_object =>
                made @ .object(fields: fields)
                closing @ true
            .nothing   => made @ .nothing
            .boolean b => made @ .boolean(value: b.value)
            .number n  => made @ .number(value: n.value)
            .string t  => made @ .string(value: t.value)

        if closing
            # The array or object around the one just closed is the innermost
            # again, and the stacks let go of their copy of it, so it is held
            # once and grows in place.
            last = outer_items.len() - 1
            items @ outer_items[last]
            fields @ outer_fields[last]
            name @ outer_names[last]
            in_object @ outer_objects[last]
            outer_items @ outer_items.slice(from: 0, to: last)
            outer_fields @ outer_fields.slice(from: 0, to: last)
            outer_names @ outer_names.slice(from: 0, to: last)
            outer_objects @ outer_objects.slice(from: 0, to: last)

        if outer_items.len() == 0
            whole @ made
        else if in_object
            fields[name] @ made
        else
            items @ items.push(made)

# `v` as JSON text, compact, with an object's fields in the order of their
# names' bytes and each number laid out as Go's `encoding/json` lays it out:
# `300`, `0.5`, `1e+21`. Two equal values write one text, and each number's
# digits read back as the same `f64`. It fails `not_json` where a number is
# not finite, and `too_deep` where arrays and objects nest deeper than 512, so
# `parse` reads every text `render` writes back as the same value.
function render(v: Value) -> str?
    out: [str] @= []

    # The arrays and objects open around the value being written, outermost
    # first: the values each holds, in the order they are written, an
    # object's names in the same order, whether it is an object, and how many
    # of its values are written.
    open_values: [[Value]] @= []
    open_names: [[str]] @= []
    open_objects: [bool] @= []
    open_done: [i64] @= []

    # The value to write next, where one is `pending`; where none is, the
    # innermost open array or object hands on its next value, or closes.
    next: Value @= v
    pending: bool @= true

    while true
        if pending
            pending @ false

            match next
                .nothing   => out @ out.push("null")
                .boolean b => out @ out.push(b.value.to_str())
                .number n =>
                    if !numeral.finite(n.value)
                        return fail(
                            code: NOT_JSON,
                            msg: f"the number {n.value} has no JSON text, which writes finite numbers alone"
                        )
                    out @ out.push(numeral.written(n.value))
                .string s => out @ out.push(quoted.written(s.value))
                .array a =>
                    if open_values.len() >= tokens.DEEPEST
                        return fail(
                            code: TOO_DEEP,
                            msg: f"arrays and objects nest deeper than {tokens.DEEPEST}, which `parse` would not read back"
                        )
                    out @ out.push("[")
                    unnamed: [str] = []
                    open_values @ open_values.push(a.items)
                    open_names @ open_names.push(unnamed)
                    open_objects @ open_objects.push(false)
                    open_done @ open_done.push(0)
                .object o =>
                    if open_values.len() >= tokens.DEEPEST
                        return fail(
                            code: TOO_DEEP,
                            msg: f"arrays and objects nest deeper than {tokens.DEEPEST}, which `parse` would not read back"
                        )
                    out @ out.push("{")
                    names = sort(keys(o.fields))
                    values: [Value] @= []

                    # Every name is one `keys` gave, so each is there.
                    for one in names
                        values @ values.push(o.fields[one].must())

                    open_values @ open_values.push(values)
                    open_names @ open_names.push(names)
                    open_objects @ open_objects.push(true)
                    open_done @ open_done.push(0)

            continue

        if open_values.len() == 0
            return ok(out.join(""))
        last = open_values.len() - 1
        done = open_done[last]

        if done < open_values[last].len()
            if done > 0
                out @ out.push(",")

            if open_objects[last]
                out @ out.push(quoted.written(open_names[last][done]))
                out @ out.push(":")
            next @ open_values[last][done]
            pending @ true
            open_done[last] @ done + 1
            continue

        if open_objects[last]
            out @ out.push("}")
        else
            out @ out.push("]")
        open_values @ open_values.slice(from: 0, to: last)
        open_names @ open_names.slice(from: 0, to: last)
        open_objects @ open_objects.slice(from: 0, to: last)
        open_done @ open_done.slice(from: 0, to: last)

# The value of the field `key` of the object `v`. It fails `not_an_object`
# where `v` is no object, and `missing_key` where the object has no field
# `key`: a field that holds `null` is there, `nothing`, and is not missing.
function field(v: Value, key: str) -> Value?
    match v
        .object o =>
            got = o.fields[key]

            if got.is_err()
                return fail(code: "missing_key", msg: "the object has no field " + quoted.written(key))
            return got
        .nothing | .boolean _ | .number _ | .string _ | .array _ =>
            found = match v
                .nothing   => "null"
                .boolean _ => "a boolean"
                .number _  => "a number"
                .string _  => "a string"
                .array _   => "an array"
                .object _  => "an object"

            return fail(
                code: NOT_AN_OBJECT,
                msg: "asked for the field " + quoted.written(key) + " of " + found + ", which is no object"
            )

## Tests
#
# A face declares only what a program calls (`examples/packages/README.md`
# § 1.2), so its tests write each call out and lean on no helper of their own,
# which the card would list beside `parse`.

test "json: the six kinds are read"
    assert parse("null").must() == .nothing
    assert parse("true").must() == .boolean(value: true)
    assert parse(" false ").must() == .boolean(value: false)
    assert parse("-2.5").must() == .number(value: -2.5)
    assert parse("\"caffè\"").must() == .string(value: "caffè")
    assert parse("[]").must() == .array(items: [])
    empty: {str: Value} = {}
    assert parse("{}").must() == .object(fields: empty)

test "json: arrays and objects hold values, an object's names in the text's order"
    doc = parse("{\"b\": [1, {\"c\": null}], \"a\": true}").must()

    match doc
        .object o =>
            assert keys(o.fields) == ["b", "a"]
            assert o.fields["a"].must() == .boolean(value: true)
            assert o.fields["b"].must() == .array(items: [.number(value: 1.0), .object(fields: {"c": .nothing})])
        .nothing | .boolean _ | .number _ | .string _ | .array _ => assert false

    assert parse("[[[]]]").must() == .array(items: [.array(items: [.array(items: [])])])

test "json: a name written twice keeps the value written last"
    assert parse("{\"a\": 1, \"a\": 2}").must() == .object(fields: {"a": .number(value: 2.0)})

test "json: a text that is not JSON fails with the face's codes, and says where"
    for text in ["", " ", "nul", "[1,]", "{\"a\" 1}", "01", "\"a", "1 2", "{\"a\":1", "[1}", "'a'"]
        match parse(text)
            .ok _  => assert false
            .err e => assert e.code == NOT_JSON

    match parse("{\n  \"a\": tru\n}")
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.msg == "expected a value, found `t` at line 2, column 8"

    match parse(repeat("[", 513))
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == TOO_DEEP

    match parse("1e400")
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "does_not_fit"

    match parse("\"\\ud800\"")
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "not_text"

    assert !parse(repeat("[", 512) + repeat("]", 512)).is_err()

test "json: a value is rendered compact, its names in byte order"
    assert render(parse(" [ 1 , true , null ] ").must()).must() == "[1,true,null]"
    nested = parse("{\"b\": 1, \"a\": {\"d\": [], \"c\": {}}}").must()
    assert render(nested).must() == "{\"a\":{\"c\":{},\"d\":[]},\"b\":1}"
    assert render(.string(value: "say \"hi\"\n")).must() == "\"say \\\"hi\\\"\\n\""
    assert render(.number(value: 300.0)).must() == "300"
    assert render(.number(value: 0.1)).must() == "0.1"
    assert render(.array(items: [])).must() == "[]"

test "json: what render writes, parse reads back as the same value"
    for text in ["null", "[1.5,-0,\"\\u0001\",{\"x\":[{}]}]", "{\"\":\"\",\"é\":1e-7}", "123456789012345680000"]
        doc = parse(text).must()
        assert parse(render(doc).must()).must() == doc

test "json: render refuses what no JSON text writes"
    zero = 0.0

    match render(.array(items: [.number(value: zero / zero)]))
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_JSON

    assert render(.number(value: 1.0 / zero)).is_err()
    deep: Value @= .nothing

    for _ in range(from: 0, to: 512)
        deep @ .array(items: [deep])

    assert render(deep).must().len() == 1028

    match render(.object(fields: {"a": deep}))
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == TOO_DEEP

test "json: a field is read from an object, and is missing or asked of no object"
    doc = parse("{\"title\": \"Heroes\", \"successor\": null}").must()
    assert field(doc, "title").must() == .string(value: "Heroes")
    assert field(doc, "successor").must() == .nothing

    match field(doc, "nothing_here")
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == "missing_key" && e.msg == "the object has no field \"nothing_here\""

    match field(.array(items: []), "title")
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_AN_OBJECT && e.msg == "asked for the field \"title\" of an array, which is no object"

## Admission
#
# Each function is here because a program of `examples/` wrote it by hand
# (the conventions, `examples/packages/README.md` § 7), counted on 2026-11-10
# by what the functions do: `grep -rli json examples --include='*.hero'
# --exclude-dir=heroes.packages` named seven files of four programs, and read,
# `binarytrees`, `interpreter` and `query` name `json/` in a comment alone, so
# `examples/json/` is the one program that reads and writes JSON.
#
# - `parse`: `parse.read`, 419 lines in its `parse.hero`, which left the
#   program with core 0.2.0 (`examples/json/parse.hero` until 2026-11-10),
#   wrong on these inputs, run on 2026-11-10: `0.3` read as
#   `0.30000000000000004`, and `123456789012345678901234567890` as
#   `1.2345678901234566e+29` where the nearest `f64` is
#   `1.2345678901234568e+29`, since it built a number a digit at a time,
#   rounding at each; `1e309` read as an infinity its renderer then wrote as
#   `inf`, which RFC 8259's grammar has no form for; `01` and a string holding
#   a raw line feed accepted, which RFC 8259 refuses; and `\u00e9` refused,
#   with `\r`, `\b` and `\f`, which a Heroes string holds (spec § 2).
# - `render`: `value.render` (`examples/json/value.hero` until 2026-11-10),
#   whose `quote` wrote a carriage return and every control character but
#   `\n` and `\t` raw, run on 2026-11-10.
# - `field`: `value.field`, whose hand form is the match the doc above
#   describes, four lines, and easy to get subtly wrong: `.default(.nothing)`
#   reports a field holding `null` as a missing one.
#
# Not adopted, and what a program writes instead:
#
# - `size` and `depth`, the json program's own report on a document: no other
#   program asks them, and each is the walk a program writes over a `Value`, a
#   `match` with an arm per kind, which `examples/json/main.hero` keeps.
# - An indented `render`, Go's `json.MarshalIndent`: no program writes one.
